# Simple Troubleshooting Summary

The iPhone was having two main problems: the battery was draining quickly, and the phone was not charging reliably when a Lightning cable was connected.

First, I tested the charging port with a known-good cable to make sure the problem was not caused by the cable. The phone still had trouble charging, which pointed to an issue with the charging port.

Next, I checked the iPhone's Battery Health. The battery capacity was at 75%, which showed that the battery had significantly degraded and was no longer holding power as well as it should.

To confirm the problems, I tested the phone with a known-good replacement battery and charging flex cable. The phone then charged normally and maintained power properly.

The troubleshooting showed that both the battery and Lightning flex cable were contributing to the problem. Replacing these components restored normal charging and battery performance.

## What I Learned

This repair demonstrated the importance of testing before replacing parts. Using a known-good cable and replacement components helped identify the actual faulty parts instead of assuming the problem was caused by the phone itself.
